        <p>
          You ask a question. Thirty hands could theoretically go up, but only
          four ever actually do — and they're the same four every time. Call on
          someone else and the room goes tense; call on the usual four and the
          rest of the class has quietly checked out.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's one of teaching's oldest, most stubborn problems. And a
          <strong>free spin the wheel tool</strong> turns out to be one of the
          simplest fixes available. It sounds almost too small to matter, but
          teachers who try it tend to keep using it — the shift in classroom
          energy is noticeable within days.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you've never used one before, our
          <a href="/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool" class="ilink-blue"
            >guide to what a spin the wheel tool is and how it works</a
          >
          covers the basics in a few minutes. In short: it's a free,
          browser-based randomiser — load it with names or options, and it picks
          one with a spin. No app, no account, no cost.
        </p>

        <figure class="ai-image">
