          <p>
            A spin wheel takes the "who do I call on" decision out of the
            teacher's hands entirely — which makes the classroom feel fairer,
            more exciting, and genuinely more fun for everyone in the room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="why-it-works">
          1. Why a Spinning Wheel Works So Well in a Classroom
        </h2>
        <p>
          There's a reason spinning things have always held children's attention
          — tops, roundabouts, fidget spinners. Watching something spin and slow
          down, not knowing exactly where it'll land, is quietly compelling in a
          way that's hard to manufacture with most other classroom tools.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's not just novelty, though. When students know that any of them
          could be picked at any moment, purely at random, their attention
          shifts. They stay a bit more alert. They start half-preparing answers
          even when they're not sure they'll be asked. That move from passive
          listener to quietly-ready participant is something a lot of teachers
          notice within the first week or two of using a wheel regularly.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's also a fairness element worth naming. Students — particularly
          older ones — notice exactly who gets called on most. A visible,
          transparent random picker removes any suspicion of favouritism from
          the equation entirely. The wheel picked, not the teacher. That small
          shift in perception does real work for classroom trust.
        </p>

        <div class="stat-strip">
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">500</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >Max custom names or prompts per wheel via single entry or Excel bulk paste</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">2s–20s</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >Adjustable spin speed (5s default) — quick warm-ups or dramatic draw reveals</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">&lt;60s</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >Setup time — from pasting a class roster to spinning fullscreen</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="box box-tip">
          <div class="box-label">🏫 Classroom facilitation takeaway</div>
          <p>
            Give students ten seconds to jot down one thought or turn to a partner
            before tapping Spin—combining visible random selection with quiet
            wait-time keeps reluctant speakers calm instead of putting anyone on
            the spot cold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          If you're curious about the reasoning behind why random selection
          works so well psychologically, our piece on
