            >the psychology behind random decision making</a
          >
          goes into more depth — worth a read for anyone interested in the "why"
          behind the tool.
        </p>

        <h2 id="getting-started">2. Getting Started in Under Two Minutes</h2>
        <p>
          This is genuinely one of the fastest tools you'll ever add to your
          teaching routine. Here's the whole process:
        </p>
        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Open the tool on any device</strong>
              <p>
                Visit
                <a href="/" class="ilink-green">spinthewheelsonline.com</a>
                on a laptop, tablet, or interactive whiteboard. No download or
                login — it loads instantly in any browser.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Add your class list</strong>
              <p>
                Type or bulk-paste student names into the entry panel (up to 500
                names per wheel). Rosters stay strictly inside your browser rather
                than uploading to any remote database.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Save the wheel for reuse</strong>
              <p>
                Once your class list is loaded, bookmark the link so you don't
                have to retype names every lesson. Set it up once, use it all
                term.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Project it for the whole room</strong>
              <p>
                Connect to your classroom projector or interactive whiteboard.
                Fullscreen mode makes it large, clear, and impossible to miss
                from the back row.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Spin, remove, repeat</strong>
              <p>
                Turn on "remove after spin" so each student is picked only once
                per round — guaranteeing everyone participates before anyone
                goes twice, automatically.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="box box-info">
          <div class="box-label">ℹ️ Teacher Tip</div>
          <p>
            Keep a separate saved wheel for each class you teach. Three groups,
            three bookmarked links, each pre-loaded — switching between them
            takes seconds between lessons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="ten-ideas">3. Ten Classroom Uses Teachers Rely On</h2>
        <p>
          A spin wheel isn't just for picking names — that's really only the
          starting point. Here are ten ways teachers are actually using one
          right now:
        </p>

        <div class="idea-grid">
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">01</div>
            <h3>Random Question Time</h3>
            <p>
              Ask the question first, give the class 30 seconds to think, then
              spin. Participation rises almost immediately because everyone's
              had a moment to prepare.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">02</div>
            <h3>Group Generator</h3>
            <p>
              Add student names and spin repeatedly to build random groups.
              Students accept random groupings far more readily than
              teacher-assigned ones.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">03</div>
            <h3>Topic Selector</h3>
            <p>
              Load essay topics or project themes onto the wheel. Whatever it
              lands on is the brief — no more debating who got the "better"
              topic.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">04</div>
            <h3>Class Job Rotation</h3>
            <p>
              Add classroom roles — board cleaner, register collector, line
              leader — and spin weekly. No favourites, fully transparent.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">05</div>
            <h3>Vocabulary Revision</h3>
            <p>
              Add words to the wheel. Whoever it lands on defines it, uses it in
              a sentence, or translates it — quick, low-pressure, high
              engagement.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">06</div>
            <h3>Presentation Order</h3>
            <p>
              Spin to set the order for student presentations. Nobody can
              complain about going first — the wheel decided, not you.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">07</div>
            <h3>Homework Review Roulette</h3>
            <p>
              Put homework questions on the wheel. Since any question could come
              up, reviewing all of it suddenly feels worthwhile to students.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">08</div>
            <h3>Exit Ticket Selector</h3>
            <p>
              Spin at the end of a lesson to pick who shares their takeaway. A
              calm, focused way to close a session.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">09</div>
            <h3>Reward Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Load small rewards — free time, pick the next song, skip a quiz
              question — and use it as a class motivator tied to achievement.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="ic-num">10</div>
            <h3>Prize Draw / Raffle</h3>
            <p>
              Use it as a class raffle for end-of-term prizes. For a fuller
              walkthrough, see our guide on
              <a href="/blogs/school-raffle-spin-wheel" class="ilink-green"
                >running a school raffle with a free spin wheel</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="box box-success">
          <div class="box-label">✅ Bonus Idea</div>
          <p>
            Build a "challenge wheel" for early finishers, loaded with extension
            tasks — "write three more examples," "sketch a diagram," "explain
            this to a partner." Fast workers spin for their next task instead of
            sitting idle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="by-subject">4. Ideas Sorted by Subject</h2>
        <p>
          The tool's flexibility means it genuinely fits every subject area. A
          few subject-specific starting points:
        </p>

        <div class="subject-grid">
          <div class="subject-chip">📖 English / Literacy</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🔢 Maths</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🔬 Science</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🌍 Geography</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">📜 History</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🎨 Art</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🌐 Languages / MFL</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">🎵 Music</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">⚽ PE / Sport</div>
          <div class="subject-chip">💡 PSHE / Citizenship</div>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>English:</strong> Load writing prompts, story genres, or
          character types. Students spin to get their creative brief, which
          removes the "I don't know what to write about" stall entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Maths:</strong> Load number concepts or problem types. A
          student spins, lands on "fractions," and answers or demonstrates on
          the spot. Fast-paced and useful for keeping revision energy up.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Languages (MFL):</strong> Load target-language vocabulary.
          Students spin and translate, use it in a sentence, or conjugate a
          related verb — good for oral practice without the awkwardness of
          having to volunteer.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Science:</strong> Load concepts, variables, or hypothesis
          statements. Groups spin for their investigation focus — takes the
          tedium out of topic selection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>History/Geography:</strong> Load key dates, events, countries,
          or figures. Spin to decide which topic a student summarises from
          memory — a low-stakes warm-up that works well before a test.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-purple">
          <div class="box-label">🎯 Cross-Curricular Tip</div>
          <p>
            Keep a "wildcard" wheel stocked with quick activities from multiple
            subjects — ideal for form time, cover lessons, or the last ten
            minutes of a Friday afternoon when energy is low and you need
            something engaging fast.
          </p>
        </div>

