        <h2 id="tips">5. Habits That Make It Work Every Time</h2>
        <p>
          A spin wheel only performs as well as the habits built around it. A
          few practices separate teachers who get lasting results from those who
          try it once and drop it:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Ask before you spin.</strong> Put the question to the class
            first, give everyone 20–30 seconds to think, then spin. Even
            hesitant students get a moment to prepare, so whoever's picked is
            far more likely to answer confidently.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use "remove after spin" as a default.</strong> In any
            session where you want full-class participation, keep it on. It
            stops the same student being picked twice before everyone else has
            had a turn.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make the spin visible.</strong> Project it every time. The
            visual is half the value — a shared moment of tension as the wheel
            slows down is something a plain name list simply can't replicate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Introduce it early in the term.</strong> Bring it in during
            the first week so it becomes a familiar part of your routine.
            Students who grow used to it from day one respond far better than
            those who meet it mid-year as a surprise.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Don't overuse it.</strong> Three or four spins per lesson is
            plenty. Reaching for it on every single decision dilutes the
            excitement — save it for moments where the drama actually adds
            something.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pair it with genuine praise.</strong> When the wheel picks
            someone and they answer well, say so. "That's a great answer — and
            you had no idea you'd be picked!" builds confidence and turns the
            next spin into something students look forward to rather than dread.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="common-mistakes">
          6. Common Mistakes — And How to Sidestep Them
        </h2>
        <p>
          Most teachers see results almost immediately. A handful of common
          missteps, though, can quietly blunt the effect:
        </p>

        <div class="info-grid">
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">❌</div>
            <h3>Spinning without warning</h3>
            <p>
              Springing a spin with no prep time causes anxiety, not engagement.
              Ask the question first, then spin.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">❌</div>
            <h3>Dropping it after two weeks</h3>
            <p>
              It only becomes a lasting habit if you use it consistently. Build
              it into your lesson structure so it's routine, not a novelty.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">❌</div>
            <h3>Only using it for names</h3>
            <p>
              Names are just the start. Teachers who get the most from it also
              use it for topics, tasks, rewards, and grouping.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">❌</div>
            <h3>Not saving the wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Retyping your class list every lesson defeats the point. Save the
              link once and it's one click away every time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

